Compare quantum predictions with counts losses and alternative preparation accounts (@physics-quantum-observations: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-quantum-preparation-measurement-order-coherence-and-observed-counts.lingua#r_00385A80F5644BEA5DBB42BCBC") { r_00A8DFF14011FB6266AF5D3CFB
A Born probability is not an observed count. With N independent repetitions of the same preparation and a fixed ideal two-outcome setting, let K count plus outcomes. The binomial model gives E[K]=Np and Var(K)=Np(1−p). The observed fraction K/N has variance p(1−p)/N for N>0. Independence, stable preparation and a fixed observation rule are assumptions to justify. Source drift, correlated trials, changing settings or adaptive exclusions can invalidate this simple law.

The exact `count_law` reuses [Investigate finite reservoirs fluctuations and distinct relaxation laws](Investigate-finite-reservoirs-fluctuations-and-distinct-relaxation-laws.lingua#r_0072F92BB90961036B562C796E)'s canonical combinatorial polynomial at ratio p/(1−p), handling p=1 separately. The reused polynomial is binomial mathematics; this does not assign a temperature or thermal equilibrium to the experiment. The checker compares it with independent repeated convolution of Bernoulli probabilities and verifies means/variances. Use existing inquiry methods for confidence claims rather than treating a finite frequency as the exact probability.

Losses can change conditional frequencies. In a simple detector model, a true plus result is retained with efficiency η₊ and a true minus with η₋, with no background or misclassification. From input probability p, recorded plus, recorded minus and missing masses are η₊p, η₋(1−p), and 1−η₊p−η₋(1−p). Among detections, the plus fraction is η₊p/[η₊p+η₋(1−p)] when the denominator is positive. For p=1/2,η₊=1,η₋=1/3, it is 3/4, although the ideal state predicts equal outcomes. One-third of inputs are missing. Conditioning on detected events can therefore mimic a different state if efficiencies are ignored.

A competing account with p=3/4 and equal efficiencies 2/3 gives the same three masses 1/2,1/6,1/3. Even retaining the total loss count does not identify state and unequal efficiencies simultaneously in this example. Independent detector calibration or additional justified settings/preparations are needed. Reuse [Bound incomplete outcomes without inventing the missing evidence](../../inquiry/core/Bound-incomplete-outcomes-without-inventing-the-missing-evidence.lingua#r_0033E9B1A78704DD440C7DB719), [Use competing explanations to choose informative observations](../../inquiry/core/Use competing explanations to choose informative observations.lingua#r_0077B280D63642B767F8BF007D) where applicable, and the ordinary distinction between an identified parameter and a fitted number. This is a benign exact counterexample, not a claim about a particular apparatus.

To compare a coherent state with a mixture, specify at least one setting whose predicted probabilities differ and keep preparation and detector models explicit. For Φ versus the classical correlated lottery, z counts alone are insufficient, while ideal x correlations discriminate. Real evidence must also account for background, setting accuracy, drift and selection. Preregistering a comparison and preserving rejected/out-of-window events can make the account easier to criticize; it does not automatically remove bias.

**Work:** derive the loss equations and the competing account, define original-input and detected denominators, and design a discrimination/calibration strategy without claiming to have performed it. State what assumptions a finite sample can challenge and which require other evidence. An exact computational match to the stipulated probability law is evidence about the implementation, not empirical confirmation of quantum mechanics or the chosen preparation.

**Continue:** [Investigate quantum preparation measurement order coherence and observed counts](Investigate-quantum-preparation-measurement-order-coherence-and-observed-counts.lingua#r_00385A80F5644BEA5DBB42BCBC).

